TI  - Oral immunotherapy in children with Cow's milk allergy: Clinical efficacy and 
      psychosocial impact in a randomized controlled trial.
PG  - 105198
LID - S2405-4577(26)02295-3 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.clnesp.2026.105198 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Cow's milk allergy (CMA) is a common food allergy in children and may 
      substantial affect health, nutrition, and family quality of life (QoL). Oral 
      immunotherapy (OIT) has emerged as a promising therapeutic approach for inducing 
      desensitization; however, data on its effectiveness and impact on family QoL in 
      Indonesia remain limited. This study aimed to evaluate the effectiveness of cow's 
      milk OIT in achieving clinical desensitization, assess immunological changes in 
      allergic reactivity, and analyze its impact on the QoL of families of children 
      with CMA. METHODS: This randomized, controlled, parallel-group clinical trial 
      included children aged 1-12 years with IgE-mediated CMA. Participants were 
      randomly assigned to either the cow's milk OIT group or the control group. The 
      primary outcome was clinical desensitization, defined as the ability to tolerate 
      150-200 mL of cow's milk. Secondary outcomes included changes in skin prick test 
      (SPT) wheal diameter and impact on family QoL as measured using the Food Allergy 
      Impact Scale (FAIS). RESULTS: Clinical desensitization was achieved by 84% of 
      participants in the OIT group (21/25), whereas no participants in the control 
      group achieved desensitization (p < 0.001). The mean final tolerated dose was 
      significantly higher in the OIT group than in the control group (188.4 +/- 27.53 mL 
      vs 46.6 +/- 6.73 mL; p < 0.001), with a mean time to desensitization of 22.24 +/- 
      2.96 weeks. SPT wheal diameter decreased significantly in the OIT group (from 
      5.24 +/- 0.78 mm to 3.81 +/- 0.88 mm; p < 0.001), with no significant change in the 
      control group (p = 0.614). Allergic reactions occurred in 70% of OIT 
      participants; however, most were mild to moderate and were managed without 
      serious adverse events. Rhinorrhea was more frequent in the OIT group (60% vs 0%; 
      p < 0.001). Total FAIS scores decreased significantly in the OIT group (z = 
      -4.947; p < 0.001), with improvements across most domains. MANOVA demonstrated a 
      strong effect of OIT across all FAIS domains (Pillai's Trace = 0.711-0.892; F = 
      57.846-193.774; p < 0.001). A history of eczema was negatively associated with 
      desensitization success (OR = 0.059; 95% CI = 0.010-0.358; p = 0.002), whereas 
      child age was positively associated with success (OR = 4.893; 95% CI = 
      1.171-20.443; p = 0.030). CONCLUSION: Cow's milk OIT effectively improved 
      clinical tolerance, reduced allergic reactivity, and enhanced family QoL among 
      children with CMA. These findings support the implementation of OIT in clinical 
      practice in Indonesia through individualized treatment and structured monitoring.
