IS  - 1750-7448 (Electronic)
IS  - 1750-743X (Print)
IS  - 1750-743X (Linking)
VI  - 12
IP  - 12
DP  - 2020 Aug
TI  - Sublingual immunotherapy for food allergy and its future directions.
PG  - 921-931
LID - 10.2217/imt-2020-0123 [doi]
AB  - Food allergy is an important medical problem with increasing prevalence 
      throughout the world. Different approaches of food immunotherapy are being 
      investigated including oral, epicutaneous and sublingual routes. Sublingual 
      immunotherapy (SLIT) for food allergy involves placement of glycerinated allergen 
      under the tongue daily to achieve allergen-specific desensitization. SLIT has 
      been studied in the treatment of hazelnut, peach, apple, milk and peanut 
      allergies with substantial focus on the treatment of peanut allergy. Phase II 
      studies have shown SLIT for treatment of peanut allergy increases the tolerated 
      dose of peanut by a substantial margin with fewer and less severe side effects 
      than other modalities. This review discusses the mechanisms of SLIT, early 
      studies of its use in food allergy and larger randomized controlled trials for 
      treatment of peanut allergy. Future directions using the mechanisms involved in 
      SLIT include oral mucosal immunotherapy for peanut allergy.
